{"parentUuid":"85df0e20-5e6f-4993-9d94-3287f81209ae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a73c288aed7cd9b98","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01NQCUAUhwTabiWj9BvbwxSk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LAwvvofxrPH2GzTnLUVTRE","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Leanstral Mistral Lean 4 agent formal proof 2026"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5741,"cache_read_input_tokens":26948,"output_tokens":311,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5741},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":311,"cache_read_input_tokens":26948,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5741,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5741,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoxqwYboQZARfeD99pGG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"38bfcdd1-153b-4bd8-8753-41be2a472d55","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:47.432Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"58aa83c5-126e-4d6a-ac28-bf56955ecb33","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a73c288aed7cd9b98","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WSyntftwSfJ85ZUBm1n8Kw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"HILBERT\" theorem prover Lean miniF2F PutnamBench 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22819\"},{\"title\":\"Hilbert: Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.22819v1\"},{\"title\":\"HILBERT: RECURSIVELY BUILDING FORMAL PROOFS ...\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=GN8OdkTo3B\"},{\"title\":\"Hilbert: Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning [Quick Review]\",\"url\":\"https://liner.com/review/hilbert-recursively-building-formal-proofs-with-informal-reasoning\"},{\"title\":\"miniF2F-Dafny: LLM-Guided Mathematical Theorem Proving via Auto-Active Verification\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.10187\"},{\"title\":\"[2509.22819] Hilbert: Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22819\"},{\"title\":\"Hilbert: Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning - Apple Machine Learning Research\",\"url\":\"https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/hilbert\"},{\"title\":\"GÖDEL’S POETRY Kelly J. Davis Unaffiliated kdavis@alum.mit.edu ABSTRACT\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.14252\"},{\"title\":\"Hilbert: Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=ljAHonPrs1\"},{\"title\":\"Gödel’s Poetry\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.14252\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about HILBERT:\n\n## Overview\n\nHILBERT is an agentic framework that bridges the gap between informal reasoning and formal verification by combining the complementary strengths of these two paradigms.\n\n## Performance\n\nHILBERT achieves state-of-the-art performance on miniF2F with pass rates of 90.8% to 99.2%, and on the challenging PutnamBench dataset, HILBERT with Gemini 2.5 Pro achieves 70.0% pass rate, nearly 20 percentage points above previous methods. HILBERT reaches 99.2% pass rate on miniF2F (6.6 points above the best public method) and solves 462/660 PutnamBench problems (70.0%), outperforming proprietary systems like SeedProver (50.4%).\n\n## Architecture and Components\n\nThe system orchestrates four components: an informal LLM that excels at mathematical reasoning, a specialized prover LLM optimized for Lean 4 tactics, a formal verifier, and a semantic theorem retriever.\n\n## Key Methodology\n\nFor each subgoal, HILBERT employs a two-stage approach: attempting formal proof generation with the prover, then falling back to the reasoner augmented with retrieved theorems. When both stages fail, the system recursively decomposes problematic subgoals into smaller problems.\n\nThe search results indicate this is a recent advancement in formal theorem proving presented as an ICLR 2026 paper.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e03143f9-92e1-4c19-a6f7-31c8008e4ad3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:51.862Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"58aa83c5-126e-4d6a-ac28-bf56955ecb33","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"38bfcdd1-153b-4bd8-8753-41be2a472d55","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"88dcafdf-a08f-4678-84c0-930bce6a1767","agentId":"a73c288aed7cd9b98","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LAwvvofxrPH2GzTnLUVTRE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Leanstral Mistral Lean 4 agent formal proof 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Leanstral: Open-Source foundation for trustworthy vibe-coding | Mistral AI\",\"url\":\"https://mistral.ai/news/leanstral\"},{\"title\":\"Leanstral - Mistral AI | Mistral Docs\",\"url\":\"https://docs.mistral.ai/models/leanstral-26-03\"},{\"title\":\"Mistral AI Launches Leanstral Open-Source Proof Agent for Lean 4 | MEXC News\",\"url\":\"https://www.mexc.com/news/941903\"},{\"title\":\"Mistral Leanstral: AI That Proves Its Own Code | AI Automation Global\",\"url\":\"https://aiautomationglobal.com/blog/mistral-leanstral-formal-code-verification-2026\"},{\"title\":\"Mistral's new agent proofs your code on the cheap • The Register\",\"url\":\"https://www.theregister.com/2026/03/17/mistral_leanstral_ai_code_verification_tool/\"},{\"title\":\"Leanstral: Mistral’s Open-Source Proof Agent for Lean 4\",\"url\":\"https://rits.shanghai.nyu.edu/ai/leanstral-mistrals-open-source-proof-agent-for-lean-4/\"},{\"title\":\"Leanstral: Mistral's Open-Source AI for Trustworthy Coding - Webkul Blog\",\"url\":\"https://webkul.com/blog/mistrals-leanstral/\"},{\"title\":\"Mistral AI Models 2026: Small 4, Large 3, Voxtral TTS, Forge — Complete Guide | Serenities AI\",\"url\":\"https://serenitiesai.com/articles/mistral-ai-models-2026-complete-guide\"},{\"title\":\"Mistral Releases Leanstral, First Open-Source Lean 4 Code Agent - NervNow™\",\"url\":\"https://nervnow.com/mistral-releases-leanstral-first-open-source-lean-4-code-agent/\"},{\"title\":\"Leanstral: Mistral AI's Open-Source Agent for Formal Proofs | AIToolly\",\"url\":\"https://aitoolly.com/ai-news/article/2026-03-17-leanstral-mistral-ais-open-source-agent-for-trustworthy-coding-and-formal-proof-engineering\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about Leanstral, Mistral AI's Lean 4 agent for formal proof:\n\n## Overview\n\nLeanstral is the first open-source code agent designed for Lean 4. Mistral AI released Leanstral on March 16, 2026, making it a 2026 release.\n\n## Architecture and Design\n\nLeanstral uses a highly sparse Mixture-of-Experts architecture — 120B total parameters with only 6B active per token. Rather than wrapping a generalist LLM around Lean's syntax, Mistral trained a specialized model to operate natively in realistic formal repositories, understanding Lean's type system, tactic language, and proof obligations from the ground up.\n\n## Performance and Benchmarks\n\nMistral benchmarks Leanstral for completing all formal proofs and correctly defining new mathematical concepts in each PR to the FLT project, instead of isolated mathematical problems. Leanstral pass@2 reaches a score of 26.3, beating Sonnet by 2.6 points, while costing only $36 to run, compared to Sonnet's $549.\n\n## Capabilities\n\nLeanstral supports arbitrary MCPs through vibe, and was specifically trained to achieve maximal performance with the frequently used lean-lsp-mcp. The model can handle tasks like proof debugging, cross-language translation, and version adaptation for newer Lean releases.\n\n## Availability\n\nLeanstral has been released with open weights (Apache 2.0) as an agent mode within Mistral Vibe, and via a free API endpoint.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"557cb51f-70aa-48cd-b03e-6f0b1e6f4669","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:55.368Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"38bfcdd1-153b-4bd8-8753-41be2a472d55","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"85df0e20-5e6f-4993-9d94-3287f81209ae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"88dcafdf-a08f-4678-84c0-930bce6a1767","agentId":"a73c288aed7cd9b98","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DUSacWY6ThQwrjU6w6FUBP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Claude 4.5 OpenAI o5 reasoning math proof tool use 2026 benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GPT-5.5 vs Claude Opus 4.7: Real-World Coding Performance Compared | MindStudio\",\"url\":\"https://www.mindstudio.ai/blog/gpt-55-vs-claude-opus-47-coding-comparison\"},{\"title\":\"GPT-5.5 vs Claude Opus 4.7: Full Benchmark Comparison 2026 | Framia.pro\",\"url\":\"https://framia.pro/page/en-US/news/gpt-5-5-vs-claude-opus-4-7\"},{\"title\":\"GPT-5.5 Review: Benchmarks, Pricing & Vs Claude (2026)\",\"url\":\"https://www.buildfastwithai.com/blogs/gpt-5-5-review-2026\"},{\"title\":\"7-0 wipeout: I put ChatGPT-5.5 vs Claude 4.7 through 7 impossible tests — and the results shocked me | Tom's Guide\",\"url\":\"https://www.tomsguide.com/ai/7-0-wipeout-i-put-chatgpt-5-5-and-claude-4-7-through-7-impossible-tests-and-the-results-shocked-me\"},{\"title\":\"ChatGPT vs Claude 2026: Full Comparison [Tested]\",\"url\":\"https://tech-insider.org/claude-vs-chatgpt-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Leaderboard 2026: GPT-5 vs Claude Opus 4.6 vs Gemini 3 (MMLU · GPQA · HLE · LiveCodeBench) | CodeSOTA | CodeSOTA\",\"url\":\"https://www.codesota.com/llm\"},{\"title\":\"Claude Opus 4.7 vs GPT-5.5: Which Frontier Model Is Best? | DataCamp\",\"url\":\"https://www.datacamp.com/blog/gpt-5-5-vs-claude-opus-4-7\"},{\"title\":\"GPT-5.5 API, Four Days In: Benchmarks vs Claude Opus 4.7 and Gemini 3.1 Pro\",\"url\":\"https://ofox.ai/blog/gpt-5-5-api-vs-claude-opus-gemini-3-1-flagship-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"AI Model Benchmarks May 2026 | Compare GPT-5, Claude 4.5, Gemini 2.5, Grok 4 | LM Council\",\"url\":\"https://lmcouncil.ai/benchmarks\"},{\"title\":\"GPT-5.4 vs Claude Opus 4.6 vs Gemini 2.5: Which AI Model Wins in March 2026? | AI Magicx Blog | AI Magicx\",\"url\":\"https://www.aimagicx.com/blog/gpt-5-4-vs-claude-opus-4-6-vs-gemini-2-5-comparison-2026\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding the 2026 AI model landscape with focus on reasoning, math, proof, tool use, and benchmarks:\n\n## Key 2026 Releases and Models\n\nGPT-5.5 launched on April 23, 2026, while Claude Opus 4.7 was released on April 16, 2026. Both represent the latest frontier models as of May 2026.\n\n## Performance on Reasoning and Math Benchmarks\n\nGPT-5.5 is more capable than GPT-5.4 on reasoning benchmarks, with substantial improvements to output conciseness and tool use. \n\nFor mathematical reasoning specifically, GPT-5.5 leads by 9.2 points on ARC-AGI-2 — one of the most important tests of novel reasoning. GPT-5.4 has a slight edge on formal mathematical proofs, likely because of OpenAI's heavy investment in the reasoning architecture that combines chain-of-thought with verification steps, producing cleaner logical chains.\n\n## Tool Use Performance\n\nOn Terminal-Bench 2.0 — which tests complex command-line workflows requiring planning, iteration, and tool coordination — GPT-5.5 leads by 13.3 percentage points. \n\nFor coding specifically, Claude Opus 4.7 leads SWE-bench Pro with 64.3% versus GPT-5.5's 58.6%, and if your workload involves code generation, code review, or autonomous agents modifying a codebase, Claude Opus 4.7 is still the benchmark leader.\n\n## Long-Context Performance\n\nGPT-5.5's clearest win is 74.0% versus Claude Opus 4.7's 32.2% on OpenAI's MRCR v2 benchmark at ultra-long contexts, making GPT-5.5 in a different category for summarizing large codebases and processing lengthy document corpora.\n\nNote: The search results show comparisons between GPT-5.5 and Claude Opus 4.7, but don't reference a \"Claude 4.5\" or \"OpenAI o5\" model—these don't appear to exist in the May 2026 timeframe based on available sources.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a1fb98f7-3f45-49d8-bec4-d6d1ddc80e06","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:56.181Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"85df0e20-5e6f-4993-9d94-3287f81209ae","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6a4c927a-8015-4224-86ac-8b303ff3154a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"88dcafdf-a08f-4678-84c0-930bce6a1767","agentId":"a73c288aed7cd9b98","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LTLLSNZBNhA2Uecg8kWqgS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LLM combinatorial optimization formal proof MILP integer programming 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS FOR COMBINATORIAL OPTIMIZATION: A SYSTEMATIC REVIEW\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.03637\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ai4co/awesome-fm4co: Recent research papers about Foundation Models for Combinatorial Optimization · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ai4co/awesome-fm4co\"},{\"title\":\"Large Language Model-driven Large Neighborhood Search for Large-Scale MILP Problems | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=teUg2pMrF0\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Infeasibility Aware Large Language Models for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.01455v1\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/modeling-paradigms/mixed-integer-linear-programming-milp\"},{\"title\":\"CoCo-MILP: Inter-Variable Contrastive and Intra-Constraint ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.09209\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed Integer Programming Workshop 2026\",\"url\":\"https://www.mixedinteger.org/2026/\"},{\"title\":\"Large Language Models for Combinatorial Optimization: A Systematic Review | ACM Computing Surveys\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3801961\"},{\"title\":\"Mathematical Programming Through the Lens of LLMs\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10820123/11195073.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding LLMs in combinatorial optimization, formal proofs, and MILP/integer programming in 2026:\n\n## Overview\n\nResearch is actively exploring the application of Large Language Models (LLMs) in Combinatorial Optimization (CO), with studies classified into semantic categories including the tasks performed by LLMs, the architectures of LLMs, existing datasets for evaluating LLMs in CO, and fields of application.\n\n## MILP and Integer Programming Focus\n\nThe majority of studies focus on LP and MILP (Mixed Integer Linear Programming). MILP sits at the crossroads of optimization paradigms, extending Linear Programming by allowing some decision variables to take integer or binary values, enabling the modeling of discrete choices, logical conditions, and combinatorial structures.\n\n## Recent Research Directions (2026)\n\nSeveral notable 2026 papers are exploring LLMs for combinatorial optimization:\n\n- Papers accepted at venues like ICLR 2026, AAMAS 2026, and AAAI 2026 address generalizable heuristic generation through LLMs with meta-optimization, LLM-driven decomposition agents for large-scale combinatorial optimization, and evolution of heuristic sets for automated heuristic design.\n\n- LLM-LNS proposes a Large Language Model-driven Large Neighborhood Search framework for large-scale MILP problems, with a dual-layer self-evolutionary LLM agent to automate neighborhood selection.\n\n- An infeasibility-aware framework applies LLMs to NP-hard combinatorial optimization problems through construction of training datasets containing both feasible and certifiably infeasible instances, supervised fine-tuning of a language model, and LLM-guided downstream search.\n\nRegarding formal proofs specifically, the search results focus more on practical applications and heuristic approaches rather than formal verification methodologies.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"291ece8e-658f-4701-b50f-4ec7d26d6353","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:57.765Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6a4c927a-8015-4224-86ac-8b303ff3154a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
